/*! \page page_date AnCH date library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section date_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH date library aims to provide facilities for applications manipulate date/time.
 *
 * \section date_prereq_sec Prerequisites
 *
 * None
 *
 * \section date_install_sec Installation
 *
 * CMake target \c anch::date:
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::date)
\endcode
 * See \ref install_sec "installation".
 *
 * \section date_concept_sec Usage
 *
 * \ref anch::date::DateTime stores a point in time (nanoseconds since epoch, nothing else): its broken down
 * representation ( \ref anch::date::DateFields : local time or UTC) is computed by \c fields . It is epoch by default,
 * \c DateTime::now gives the current time ; it can be built from \c std::chrono::system_clock::time_point ,
 * \c std::time_t , \c std::tm (local time), \c std::timespec and local \ref anch::date::DateFields , and explicitly
 * converted to these types. Points in time are ordered.\n
 * Local time follows the \c TZ environment variable, daylight saving time included.\n
 *\n
 * \ref anch::date::DateFormatter formats and parses dates with patterns: \c \%Y , \c \%y , \c \%m , \c \%d , \c \%H ,
 * \c \%h (12 hours), \c \%p (a.m./p.m.), \c \%M , \c \%S and \c \%s (milliseconds). Other patterns can be added by
 * implementing \ref anch::date::IDatePartFormatter (on \ref anch::date::DateFields ) and registering it with
 * \ref anch::date::DateFormatter::registerFormatterPart .\n
 * Parsing is strict: every field has a fixed size, constant parts have to match and impossible dates are refused.\n
 *\n
 * For simple formatting, \c std::format with \c std::chrono specifiers can also be used on the converted time point.\n
 *\n
 * \ref anch::date::rfc3339 formats and parses RFC 3339 dates (JSON schema and OpenAPI \c date-time and \c date
 * formats): \c date-time is a \ref anch::date::DateTime (written in UTC or with the local offset), \c full-date is a
 * \ref anch::date::Date . anch-json uses them for these types when \c ANCH_DATE is \c ON .
 *\n
 * \ref anch::date::Date is a calendar day (0000-01-01 to 9999-12-31, always valid): it is not a point in time, its
 * conversions to and from \ref anch::date::DateTime give the time zone ( \ref anch::date::Zone ). Dates are ordered,
 * days, months and years can be added:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-date/examples/date.cpp date
 *\n
 * \ref anch::date::Time is a time of day (00:00:00 to 23:59:59.999999999, RFC 3339 \c partial-time , SQL \c TIME ),
 * without time zone either:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-date/examples/date.cpp time
 *
 * \section date_example_sec Example
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-date/examples/date.cpp formatter
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-date/examples/date.cpp rfc3339
 */
